// ============================================================================
// DAILY — 2026-11-20 (Friday): Acid / psychedelia free jam — the lydian spiral
// ============================================================================
//
// Friday is taste-forward, not theory-first (CREATIVE-DIRECTION.md's 10 Sep
// correction). Checked the last three Fridays before writing this one:
// 11-13 used D hungarian minor / a hard-panned L-R call-response duel, kick
// only. 11-06 used G bebop / a real 303 slide on one centered voice, kick
// only. 10-30 used C altered / an automated VOWEL filter on one centered
// voice, kick + sparse hat. None of them used a scale with a natural major
// 3rd, and none used the "spiral arp" or "Goa kick" devices MUSIC-KNOWLEDGE.md
// §18 researched (29 Oct, after Abhiraj's Hallucinogen-comparison correction:
// "very loud, shrill... theirs are smoother, have distinct panned sounds and
// create a whole environment") but never applied to a daily pattern — only to
// the after-party set prototype.
//
//   SCALE: A lydian dominant (verified against tonaljs's own scale-type
//   source, raw.githubusercontent.com/tonaljs/tonal, packages/scale-type/
//   data.ts: "1P 2M 3M 4A 5P 6M 7M" -> wait, checked the actual line used —
//   "1P 2M 3M 4A 5P 6M 7m", aliased "lydian b7"/"overtone" — same discipline
//   as Bhairav's "gypsy" check). Mixolydian with a raised 4th: a bright,
//   major-feeling scale with one sharp tension tone (#4) and a flat 7, the
//   jazz scale played over a dominant-7#11 chord — real Berklee vocabulary,
//   not a decorative pick, and a genuinely different color from the last
//   three Fridays' minor/altered/bebop scales. Degrees: 0=A 1=B 2=C# 3=D#
//   (#4, the scale's own tension tone) 4=E (P5, this file's pivot/anchor)
//   5=F# 6=G(b7) 7=A'. 11=E' (the pivot an octave up), 13=G' (b7 an octave up).
//
//   PRODUCTION — two devices from MUSIC-KNOWLEDGE.md §18, neither tried on a
//   daily pattern before:
//   1. "Spiral arp" — a 5-note cell against the kick's 4 even pulses: true
//      quintuplets, not a 16-step grid division, so the arp drifts against
//      the downbeat on its own, no manual per-cycle shifting needed (§18:
//      "odd-length loops... so the motif seems to keep changing"). The
//      pivot (the 5th, degree 4/11) is struck every OTHER cell note and
//      stays FIXED across phrases A/B/C — an anchor the way a raga's vadi
//      anchors an improvisation, not a transposed copy of one shape — while
//      the notes around it change each phrase. That's this file's jazz-
//      improv structural device (MUSIC-QC-LOOP.md checklist).
//   2. "Goa kick" (§18: "longer than a psytrance kick, with a mid-range
//      knock and warm saturation") — a longer decay than the last three
//      Fridays' identical short click-kick formula, plus `.shape()` (light
//      waveshaper, verified in knowledge-base/strudel/learn/effects.md)
//      for the warm saturation, not just a sine+click.
//
//   WIDTH — a mirrored echo, not a centered delay (§11/§18: "distinct panned
//   sounds... each element in its own place", not everything Haas-widened
//   the same way). The dry arp sits left (.3); a SEPARATE voice plays the
//   same melody late by a dotted-8th (3/16 cycle) and carries its own delay/
//   feedback trail, panned right (.75) — an echo that itself keeps echoing,
//   not a single doubled copy. Its feedback is this file's ONE automated arc
//   (§10's "fewer independently-drifting knobs"): it swells from .3 toward
//   near self-oscillation (.82) over cycles 0-10, then snaps back to .25 on
//   cycle 11 — which lands on phrase D, the release — exactly the "swelling
//   toward near self-oscillation before a drop, snapping back" FX-as-
//   instrument move §18 describes for Goa trance delay sends.
//
//   DRUMS — kick only, synthesised (MUSIC-KNOWLEDGE.md §19: no TR808/909
//   sample bank), no hats. Checked deliberately, not by default (MUSIC-QC-
//   LOOP.md's percussion item): the quintuplet arp against the kick's
//   quarters is already the rhythmic interest this file has to offer, and a
//   hi-hat grid would just fight that polyrhythm instead of framing it — the
//   same "distinct panned sounds, not a filled top end" reasoning 10-30
//   used for its own sparse-hat choice, taken one step further here.
//   Resonance (lpq) on both arp voices is capped at 7, under the ~8 ceiling
//   MUSIC-QC-LOOP.md's failure #5 flagged as reading as whine — this is
//   meant to sit as atmosphere, not as a screaming 303.
//
//   BASS — a Goa trance split (MUSIC-KNOWLEDGE.md §6): a low, rolling sub,
//   almost entirely on the tonic with one step to the 5th per cycle, doing a
//   separate, simpler job from the melodic arp above it. Kick and bass both
//   stay centered/mono (§18: "kick and bass mono").
//
// LEAD PHRASES — a 5-note cell per cycle, rotating <A B C D> every cycle
// across 12 cycles (3 full rounds):
//   A (call, low register, states the tonic and leaps a full 5th to the
//     pivot, then neighbors it with the 3rd and the b7).
//   B (variation — DIFFERENT neighbor tones around the SAME fixed pivot:
//     the 2nd and the #4 instead of A's 3rd and b7, so the anchor reads as
//     an anchor, not a coincidence).
//   C (peak — register leap of a full octave+ up: tonic-up-an-octave to the
//     pivot-up-an-octave, the file's widest leaps).
//   D (release — tonic, rest, pivot, rest, tonic: sparse, resolving).
//
// TEMPO: setcpm(35) with a kick*4 -> BPM = 35*4 = 140, Goa/psy-techno
// territory (MUSIC-KNOWLEDGE.md §12: Goa releases run 131-147). CYCLES: 12.
// 12*60/35 = 20.6s.
// ============================================================================

setcpm(35)
let CYCLES = 12

// kick — "Goa kick" (MUSIC-KNOWLEDGE.md §18): longer decay + light waveshaper
// warmth than the last three Fridays' short click-kick, still synthesised
// (§19), tuned to A1, this file's tonic. Drives the sidechain on orbit 2.
let kick = stack(
  note("a1").s("sine").struct("x*4")
    .penv(28).pdecay(.05)
    .attack(.001).decay(.24).sustain(0).postgain(3.2)
    .shape(.22),
  s("white").struct("x*4")
    .hpf(1200).lpf(5500).attack(.0005).decay(.015).sustain(0).postgain(1.1),
)
  .duckorbit(2).duckattack(.02).duckdepth(.45)
  .gain(.55)

// bass — Goa split (§6): a rolling sub, almost entirely on the tonic, one
// step to the 5th per cycle. Centered, mono, its own separate job from the
// arp above it.
let bass = note("a1 a1 a1 e1")
  .s("sine")
  .attack(.01).decay(.3).sustain(.5).release(.08)
  .lpf(500)
  .pan(.5)
  .orbit(2)
  .gain(.38)

// the spiral arp: a 5-note cell per cycle (quintuplets against the kick's 4
// even pulses — a polyrhythm, not a shifting grid, so it drifts on its own).
// Pivot = the 5th (degree 4, or 11 an octave up) at positions 1 and 3 of
// every cell in A/B/C, fixed in place while the surrounding notes change —
// the file's structural anchor. D drops to a sparse tonic/pivot/tonic release.
let melody = n(`<
  [0 4 2 4 6]
  [1 4 6 4 3]
  [7 11 9 11 13]
  [0 ~ 4 ~ 0]
>`).scale("A3:lydian:dominant")

let leadDry = melody
  .sound("saw")
  .attack(.006).decay(.12).sustain(.08).release(.06)
  .lpf(1700).lpq(7)
  .distort(.12)
  .pan(.3)
  .gain(.3)
  .orbit(2)

// feedback arc for the echo voice only (this file's one automated parameter,
// per §10): swells toward near self-oscillation over cycles 0-10, then snaps
// back on cycle 11 — which lands on phrase D, the release (§18's "swelling
// toward self-oscillation before a drop, snapping back when the kick
// returns" FX-as-instrument move).
let fb = `<.3 .34 .38 .43 .48 .53 .58 .64 .7 .76 .82 .25>`

// the mirrored echo (§11/§18): the SAME melody, late by a dotted-8th (3/16
// cycle), carrying its own delay/feedback trail, panned to the opposite
// side from the dry arp — an echo that keeps echoing, not a single widened
// copy sitting in the same place as the dry signal.
let leadEcho = melody
  .late(3/16)
  .sound("saw")
  .attack(.006).decay(.12).sustain(.08).release(.06)
  .lpf(1300).lpq(7)
  .delay(.6).delaytime(3/16).delayfeedback(fb)
  .pan(.75)
  .gain(.16)
  .orbit(2)

stack(kick, bass, leadDry, leadEcho)
